{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789915785,"nonce":"A4SZWbVxzsKQwQMqQSMLR3KjFYo=","title":"nbody lost to hxcpp after the array-read bounds clamp","message":"CI benchmarks: rayzor-llvm nbody 703ms vs haxe-cpp 729ms through 84c057bf;\n811 vs 727 at 6113ac5e (cranelift 812 -\u003e 955). The first losing run is\n30015d6b, which made `lower_index_access` clamp out-of-range reads with a\nselect on the element address and a select on the loaded value. Both sit\nin front of every element load: the load cannot issue until the length\ncompare resolves, and the selected base hides the access from LLVM.\n\nLocal, min user time of 10 interleaved runs (busy laptop): unchecked\n(84c057bf) 0.598s, select (6113ac5e) 0.649s, branch form 0.605s. A 4M-element\nsum loop: 0.353 / 0.383 / 0.270.\n\nFix: guard the load with a conditional branch and merge the null through a\nphi. Same semantics (an index past `len` reads null/0), no compare on the\nload's path.","files":null}]}